Third-party material
====================

The material listed under the headings below -- AMD, Fairchild,
Hewlett-Packard, the firmware images, Microchip, Motorola, the Z3801A
owner's description, NIST, Philips, Texas Instruments and Xilinx -- is
not part of this project, and the project's license does not apply to
any of it.  Each document is the property of its copyright
holder.  They are included for reference because they are the
specification this software is written against: the SCPI command table
cites them by page, and a claim in the code that is not backed by a
receiver on the bench is backed by one of these.

No license to any of this material is granted or implied by its
presence here.

That material is the manuals, papers and data sheets in `hp/`,
`motorola/`, `amd/`, `fairchild/`, `microchip/`, `philips/`, `ti/`,
`nist/`, `xilinx/` and `community/`, and `firmware/images/`: the images
(`IMAGE.bin`), the console log one was rebuilt from, the EEPROM contents
in `firmware/images/eeprom/` and the FPGA bitstreams in
`firmware/images/fpga/`.  The
Ghidra archive in `firmware/ghidra/` is both: the image it holds is the
vendor's, and the names, types and comments laid over it are the
project's.

GCC's source in `gcc/` is not part of the project either, and the
project's license does not apply to it, but it is not reference
material: it is built into the rebuilt image's libgcc, and it is free
software under its own licenses, which its heading below gives and
which come with it.

The project's own work
----------------------

The rest of `firmware/` is the project's own work, its analysis of the
vendors' images, not vendor material (`firmware/README.md`).  Unlike
the rest of `third_party/`, `firmware/decompiled/`, `firmware/rewritten/`
and `firmware/analysis/` are derived from the vendor images:

  include/           the image-independent headers: the MC68331's
                     modules (mc68331.h) and the 68681 DUART's
                     (mc68681.h), from the manuals in `motorola/`, and
                     the kernel's signals (psos-signals.h), from the
                     images' own messages.
  analysis/IMAGE/    what the project learned about an image: its
                     names files (names.tsv, scpi-tree.tsv,
                     installer-scpi-tree.tsv, modules.tsv,
                     results.tsv), its SCPI paths' handlers
                     (handlers.txt), the survey of its untyped ROM
                     (untyped.tsv) and its magic-number allowlist
                     (magic-allow.tsv).
  decompiled/IMAGE/  an image's decompiled C, below.
  rewritten/IMAGE/   an image's hand-written C, below, with its type
                     header and layout check (types/smartclock.h,
                     types/check-layout.c).
  rewritten/common/  the hand-written C several images share, and the
                     types their headers share (types/).
  Makefile           the checks and generated files of those folders,
                     to which the top Makefile delegates.
  README.md          the layout.

What this work reproduces of an image -- the routines the decompiled
C carries as their bytes, the tables it and the hand-written C copy --
is the image's, and remains its copyright holder's.

Decompiled C, in `firmware/decompiled/IMAGE/`: Ghidra 12.1.4's
decompiler output for an image, produced by the scripts in
`tools/ghidra/` (`docs/firmware/README.md`, "Decompiling with Ghidra"):
machine output, named where the project's notes and tables establish a
name; the decompiler's own text is not reviewed, and each function it
reads is kept under `#if 0` beside the hand-written C that replaces it,
which is reviewed and proven against the image (`docs/firmware/
rewrite.md`).  The exporter rewrites the decompiler's text where
its C would not compile or would not do what the image does, and names
what the tables name (`docs/firmware/README.md`, "Decompiling with
Ghidra").

  z3805a-3543b/z3805a-3543b.c
                     Z3805A 3543B, every function in address order.
  z3805a-3543b/z3805a-3543b.conventions.jsonl
                     the same functions, one JSON object a line: each
                     one's note and calling convention, from the names
                     file and the scripts, or "unknown".
  z3805a-3543b/z3805a-3543b.symbols.ld
                     the addresses its assembly names outside any
                     routine, for the linker.

Hand-written C, in `firmware/rewritten/IMAGE/`: an image's functions
written by hand in place of the decompiled ones, each checked against
the image's own code (`docs/firmware/README.md`, "Hand-written C"), a
file to a module (`docs/firmware/modules.md`).

  z3805a-3543b/      Z3805A 3543B.

